# Architect review r2: comparator fold-in and doc amendment

I re-read the current diff of both source files and the doc, re-ran my r1 probes against the working tree, and ran the jing test namespace on the JVM. No files were edited.

## Finding 2 fold-in: confirmed closed

Sorting by `canonical-print` closes the crack. The tie that dropped a map entry came from the host printer truncating or reordering the sort key under an ambient binding. The sort key is now the same byte string the encoder emits, so a tie in the comparator means byte-identical elements, which the source collection could not have held as distinct keys in the first place. Re-probed on the JVM against the current tree:

| Ambient binding | r1 | r2 |
|---|---|---|
| print-length 1 on a map with vector keys | address changed | stable |
| print-meta true on a map with a metadata-bearing key | address changed | stable |
| print-level 1 on a set of nested vectors | not probed | stable |
| print-readably false on a string scalar | collides with the symbol | still collides |

The last row is the disclosed scalar residual. Deferring the default-binding wrapper to the fail-closed follow-up unit is acceptable, since it belongs with the other encoder-boundary hardening and the scalar branch is already documented as delegating to the host printer.

Set and map order-insensitivity re-verified with mixed scalar and vector members, including a map built with a reversed comparator. JVM test run: 38 tests, 227 assertions, 0 failures. I did not run the JS or Dart lanes this round. The r2 change uses no host-specific constructs, so the r1 cross-host results carry, but the orchestrator's usual three-host run before commit still applies.

## Declaration order: safe on all three hosts

Moving `canonical-print` above `order-normalize` is correct and removes a forward reference rather than creating one. The only cycles are self-recursion inside each function, which every host's `defn` supports. `order-normalize` calls `canonical-print` and itself only. `canonical-print` calls itself only. Neither refers to `order-normalized-print` or `content-hash`, which follow them. ClojureScript warns on forward references and ClojureDart rejects them in some positions, so the backward-pointing dependency is the right shape for both.

